    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Upwards of 50% of cancers exhibit deregulation of MYC, a transcription factor that controls evasion of immune system detection and other aspects of cancer growth. MYC has also been implicated in the modification of the glycolcalyx, or cellular forest of sugars (glycans), and the production of sialic acid, a specific glycan modifier. Alterations to the glycocalyx broadly characterize a variety of cancers. Certain glycans containing sialic acid (sialoglycans) are known to inhibit immune function by binding to Siglecs, a family of inhibitory receptors present on most immune cells. However, the pathways leading to elevated sialoglycan synthesis (hypersialylation) in cancer are not well characterized. In this project, we explore a possible role for oncogenes in promoting sialoglycan production by upregulating transcription of sialyltransferases, which transfer sialic acids to nascent glycans, in murine and human MYC-driven hematopoieitic cancers.\r\nChemical and genetic MYC inhibition resulted in reduced Siglec ligands and altered glycosylation on murine and human T-ALL cells. Titration of MYC broadly altered transcriptional expression of sialoglycan biosynthetic enzymes; expression of several sialyltransferases, most notably ST6GALNAC4, decreased following MYC suppression. Deletion of ST6GALNAC4 in murine and human hematopoietic cancers reduced the expression of Siglec ligands and increased susceptibility to NK cell killing. While MYC has been implicated in a variety of cancer-promoting activities, our results offer a new connection between MYC and cancer immunoevasion through the glycocalyx in hematological malignancies. While current therapies targeting MYC have been unsuccessful, a better understanding of sialoglycan biosynthesis may provide novel prognostic markers and therapeutic targets.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
